quantize_config.json` which has `is_marlin_format: true`\n- It checks for `model.safetensors` first (line 847-859 in my edit)\n- If found, it sets `is_marlin_format = True` and uses that file\n- Then it goes through the Marlin loading path (line 1090+)\n- It checks for the Marlin cache using `_get_cached_marlin_save_name`\n- If cache exists, it loads from cache (fast)\n- If not, it loads from `model.safetensors`, converts to Marlin, and caches it\n\nWait, there's an issue here. When we save a model that's already in Marlin format (with MarlinQuantLinear modules), the state dict will have Marlin-specific keys (B, s, workspace). When we load it back, we should be able to load it directly without conversion.\n\nBut the current logic assumes we're always loading a GPTQ model and converting it to Marlin. We need to handle the case where we're loading a model that's already in Marlin format.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. When we call `save_pretrained` on a model that was loaded with `use_marlin=True`:\n- The model has MarlinQuantLinear modules\n- The state dict contains Marlin-specific keys (B, s, workspace, bias)\n- We save this to `model.safetensors`\n- We save `quantize_config.json` with `is_marlin_format: true`\n\nWhen we load this back with `use_marlin=True`:\n- We detect `is_marlin_format: true` from the config\n- We load `model.safetensors`\n- We should create MarlinQuantLinear modules and load the state dict directly\n\nThe issue is that when we create the model initially, we create regular QuantLinear modules (GPTQ format), not MarlinQuantLinear. So we need to either:\n1. Create MarlinQuantLinear modules from the start when loading a Marlin format model\n2. Or convert the QuantLinear modules to MarlinQuantLinear and then load the state dict\n\nOption 2 is what the current code does, but it's inefficient because it creates the wrong modules first and then converts them.\n\nActually, looking at the code flow more carefully, I see that when `use_marlin=